{"timestamp": "2025-10-15T17:20:54.999436", "function_name": "make_stick", "function_body": "  \"\"\"\n  Generate a sequence of actions that lets the agent craft a ``stick`` in the\n  given ``CraftLab`` environment.\n\n  The implementation works for the standard CraftWorld environments that\n  expose the following attributes (as defined in the provided code base):\n\n  * ``env.world`` \u2013 the :class:`CraftWorld` instance.\n  * ``env.scenario`` \u2013 the :class:`CraftScenario` instance.\n  * ``env._current_state`` \u2013 the current :class:`CraftState` (available after\n    ``env.reset()`` or after a step).\n  * ``env.step(action)`` \u2013 executes ``action`` and updates the internal state.\n\n  Action indices follow the ordering used throughout the library::\n\n      0 \u2013 DOWN\n      1 \u2013 UP\n      2 \u2013 LEFT\n      3 \u2013 RIGHT\n      4 \u2013 USE   (collect / craft)\n\n  The planner performs three high\u2011level steps:\n\n  1.  Determine the index of the ``stick`` item.\n  2.  Retrieve the primitive ingredients required to craft a stick.\n  3.  For each required primitive, move the agent next to a cell that contains\n      the primitive, face the cell and issue a ``USE`` action to pick it up.\n  4.  Move to the appropriate workshop for the stick recipe and issue a\n      ``USE`` action to craft the stick.\n\n  The planner is deliberately simple: it uses Manhattan distance to select\n  the *nearest* instance of each required primitive, does not take obstacles\n  into account (the environments used for evaluation are obstacle\u2011free for\n  the required items), and does not adjust the agent's facing direction\n  beyond what is needed for a ``USE`` action.\n\n  Parameters\n  ----------\n  env : CraftLab (or compatible) instance\n      The environment in which the stick must be crafted.  The environment\n      should be already reset (i.e. ``env.reset()`` has been called) so that\n      ``env._current_state`` is valid.\n\n  Returns\n  -------\n  List[int]\n      A list of action indices that, when executed sequentially with\n      ``env.step(action)``, results in the agent possessing at least one\n      ``stick`` in its inventory.\n  \"\"\"\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  # Helper utilities\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  ACTIONS = {\"DOWN\": 0, \"UP\": 1, \"LEFT\": 2, \"RIGHT\": 3, \"USE\": 4}\n  DIR_VECTORS = {\n      0: (0, 1),   # DOWN  : +y\n      1: (0, -1),  # UP    : -y\n      2: (-1, 0),  # LEFT  : -x\n      3: (1, 0),   # RIGHT : +x\n  }\n\n  def manhattan(a, b):\n      return abs(a[0] - b[0]) + abs(a[1] - b[1])\n\n  def find_nearest(kind_idx, grid, start):\n      \"\"\"Return the position (x, y) of the nearest cell containing ``kind_idx``.\"\"\"\n      ys, xs = np.where(grid[:, :, kind_idx] > 0)\n      if len(xs) == 0:\n          raise ValueError(f\"No cells contain required kind {kind_idx}\")\n      # compute Manhattan distances\n      dists = np.abs(xs - start[0]) + np.abs(ys - start[1])\n      nearest_idx = np.argmin(dists)\n      return (int(xs[nearest_idx]), int(ys[nearest_idx]))\n\n  def move_to(target, state):\n      \"\"\"\n      Produce a list of movement actions that brings the agent from its\n      current position to ``target`` (exact cell).  The function does **not**\n      consider obstacles \u2013 it simply walks in x then y direction.\n      \"\"\"\n      actions = []\n      cur_x, cur_y = state.pos\n      tgt_x, tgt_y = target\n\n      # move in x\n      while cur_x != tgt_x:\n          if cur_x < tgt_x:\n              actions.append(ACTIONS[\"RIGHT\"])\n              cur_x += 1\n          else:\n              actions.append(ACTIONS[\"LEFT\"])\n              cur_x -= 1\n\n      # move in y\n      while cur_y != tgt_y:\n          if cur_y < tgt_y:\n              actions.append(ACTIONS[\"DOWN\"])\n              cur_y += 1\n          else:\n              actions.append(ACTIONS[\"UP\"])\n              cur_y -= 1\n\n      return actions\n\n  def face_towards(agent_pos, target_pos):\n      \"\"\"\n      Return a single movement action that makes the agent face the cell\n      ``target_pos``.  The environment defines the facing direction as the\n      direction of the last movement; therefore we simply move one step\n      towards the target and then step back with a ``USE`` (the ``USE`` will\n      act on the cell we just left, which is the intended primitive/workshop).\n      For simplicity we just return an empty list \u2013 the calling code will\n      invoke ``USE`` while standing next to the target.\n      \"\"\"\n      return []  # no explicit turn action needed in this simplified model\n\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  # 1. Identify the stick index and its recipe\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  world = env.world\n  cookbook = world.cookbook\n\n  # ``stick`` is a canonical name used in the standard recipe files.\n  stick_name = \"stick\"\n  stick_idx = cookbook.index.get(stick_name)\n  if stick_idx is None:\n      raise ValueError(\"The environment does not contain a recipe for 'stick'.\")\n\n  # Primitive requirements for a single stick.\n  primitive_req = cookbook.primitives_for(stick_idx)  # dict {prim_idx: count}\n  if not primitive_req:\n      # No recipe \u2013 stick is a primitive itself; just collect one.\n      primitive_req = {stick_idx: 1}\n\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  # 2. Gather required primitives\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  actions_plan = []\n  state = env._current_state  # current CraftState\n\n  for prim_idx, needed in primitive_req.items():\n      for _ in range(needed):\n          # Locate the nearest instance of the primitive.\n          prim_pos = find_nearest(prim_idx, state.grid, state.pos)\n\n          # Move to a cell *adjacent* to the primitive.\n          # For simplicity we move onto the primitive cell itself; the\n          # environment allows ``USE`` when the target cell is directly in\n          # front of the agent, so we step next to it and then use.\n          # Choose an adjacent cell (preferably horizontally aligned).\n          adj_candidates = [\n              (prim_pos[0] + dx, prim_pos[1] + dy)\n              for dx, dy in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n          ]\n          # Filter candidates inside the grid bounds.\n          H, W, _ = state.grid.shape\n          adj_candidates = [\n              (x, y)\n              for (x, y) in adj_candidates\n              if 0 <= x < W and 0 <= y < H\n          ]\n\n          # Pick the candidate with smallest distance to the agent.\n          adj_pos = min(\n              adj_candidates,\n              key=lambda p: manhattan(state.pos, p)\n          )\n\n          # Move to the adjacent position.\n          actions_plan.extend(move_to(adj_pos, state))\n          # Update internal state to reflect the moves (so subsequent\n          # distance calculations are correct).  We mimic the effect of\n          # ``env.step`` without actually calling it.\n          for a in actions_plan[-len(move_to(adj_pos, state)) :]:\n              dx, dy = DIR_VECTORS[a]\n              state = state.__class__(\n                  scenario=state.scenario,\n                  grid=state.grid,\n                  pos=(state.pos[0] + dx, state.pos[1] + dy),\n                  dir=a,\n                  inventory=state.inventory.copy(),\n              )\n\n          # Face the primitive (no explicit turn needed) and collect it.\n          actions_plan.append(ACTIONS[\"USE\"])\n          # Simulate the collection: decrement the primitive count on the\n          # grid and increment inventory.\n          px, py = prim_pos\n          state.grid[px, py, prim_idx] -= 1\n          state.inventory[prim_idx] += 1\n\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  # 3. Craft the stick at its workshop\n  # --------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n  # The workshop index is stored in the recipe under the special key \"_at\".\n  # The cookbook stores the full recipe dict; we retrieve it.\n  recipe_dict = cookbook.recipes.get(stick_idx, {})\n  workshop_idx = recipe_dict.get(\"_at\")\n  if workshop_idx is None:\n      # No specific workshop \u2013 crafting can be done anywhere.\n      # In the original environment a stick is usually craftable at the\n      # \"workbench\" workshop, but we treat the current position as sufficient.\n      actions_plan.append(ACTIONS[\"USE\"])\n      return actions_plan\n\n  # Locate a workshop cell of the required type.\n  workshop_pos = find_nearest(workshop_idx, state.grid, state.pos)\n\n  # Move adjacent to the workshop (same logic as for primitives).\n  adj_candidates = [\n      (workshop_pos[0] + dx, workshop_pos[1] + dy)\n      for dx, dy in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n  ]\n  H, W, _ = state.grid.shape\n  adj_candidates = [\n      (x, y) for (x, y) in adj_candidates if 0 <= x < W and 0 <= y < H\n  ]\n  adj_pos = min(adj_candidates, key=lambda p: manhattan(state.pos, p))\n\n  actions_plan.extend(move_to(adj_pos, state))\n  # No need to update ``state`` further \u2013 the final ``USE`` will be handled\n  # by the environment when the plan is executed.\n\n  # Finally, issue the crafting action.\n  actions_plan.append(ACTIONS[\"USE\"])\n\n  return actions_plan", "env_interactions": 0, "island_id": 9, "scores": {"3": -1}}
