{"timestamp": "2025-10-15T20:03:33.425531", "function_name": "make_stick", "function_body": "  # try to use env.plan or env.plan_action etc.\n  if hasattr(env, \"plan\"):\n      try:\n          return list(env.plan(\"stick\"))\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n  # fallback: if env.world.cookbook has index\n  try:\n      stick_idx = env.world.cookbook.index[\"stick\"]\n      # maybe env.plan_by_index\n      if hasattr(env, \"plan_by_index\"):\n          return list(env.plan_by_index(stick_idx))\n  except Exception:\n      pass\n  return []", "env_interactions": 0, "island_id": 7, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
{"timestamp": "2025-10-15T20:04:03.033331", "function_name": "make_stick", "function_body": "  \"\"\"\n  Return a list of action indices that will (in principle) collect the\n  required primitives for a ``stick`` and then craft it at the appropriate\n  workshop.\n\n  The implementation is deliberately generic \u2013 it does **not** rely on any\n  particular map layout.  It uses the recipe information stored in the\n  ``CraftWorld``/``Cookbook`` objects to discover which primitive items are\n  needed and where the workshop for the target item is located.  The agent\n  then moves in Manhattan fashion to the first occurrence of each primitive,\n  uses the ``USE`` action the required number of times, and finally moves to\n  a workshop cell and uses ``USE`` again to craft the stick.\n\n  The function assumes the following about the environment API (which holds\n  for the standard ``CraftLab`` wrapper used in the tests):\n\n  * ``env.world`` \u2013 the ``CraftWorld`` instance.\n  * ``env.scenario`` \u2013 the ``CraftScenario`` instance.\n  * ``env.scenario.init_grid`` \u2013 a ``(W, H, K)`` numpy array where\n    ``grid[x, y, k] > 0`` indicates the presence of an entity of kind ``k``\n    at cell ``(x, y)``.\n  * ``env.scenario.init_pos`` \u2013 the starting ``(x, y)`` coordinates of the\n    agent.\n  * Action indices: ``DOWN=0, UP=1, LEFT=2, RIGHT=3, USE=4`` (as defined in\n    the original source).\n\n  The returned list can be fed to ``env.step(action)`` repeatedly; the\n  function does **not** execute the actions itself.\n  \"\"\"\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Helper to convert a direction into the corresponding action index.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  ACTION_DOWN, ACTION_UP, ACTION_LEFT, ACTION_RIGHT, ACTION_USE = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Retrieve the index of the goal item (``stick``) and its recipe.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  world = env.world\n  cookbook = world.cookbook\n  stick_idx = cookbook.index[\"stick\"]                     # goal kind id\n  stick_recipe = cookbook.recipes.get(stick_idx, {})\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Determine the primitive requirements for a single stick.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  primitive_needs = cookbook.primitives_for(stick_idx)   # {kind_id: count}\n  # ``primitives_for`` already expands recursive recipes, giving us the\n  # flat list of primitive kinds that must be collected.\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Locate the workshop where a stick can be crafted.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  workshop_name = stick_recipe.get(\"_at\")\n  workshop_idx = None\n  if workshop_name is not None:\n      workshop_idx = cookbook.index[workshop_name]\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Initialise the plan.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  actions = []\n  cur_x, cur_y = env.scenario.init_pos                     # agent start\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Simple Manhattan\u2011movement helper.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  def move_to(target_x, target_y):\n      \"\"\"Append actions that move the agent from its current position to\n      ``(target_x, target_y)`` using only horizontal then vertical moves.\"\"\"\n      nonlocal cur_x, cur_y, actions\n      # Horizontal moves\n      while cur_x < target_x:\n          actions.append(ACTION_RIGHT)\n          cur_x += 1\n      while cur_x > target_x:\n          actions.append(ACTION_LEFT)\n          cur_x -= 1\n      # Vertical moves\n      while cur_y < target_y:\n          actions.append(ACTION_DOWN)\n          cur_y += 1\n      while cur_y > target_y:\n          actions.append(ACTION_UP)\n          cur_y -= 1\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Collect each required primitive.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  grid = env.scenario.init_grid\n  for prim_idx, needed_cnt in primitive_needs.items():\n      # Find *any* cell that currently contains the primitive.\n      locations = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, prim_idx] > 0)\n      if locations.size == 0:\n          # No such primitive on the map \u2013 in a well\u2011formed scenario this\n          # should never happen, but we guard against it.\n          continue\n      # Use the first discovered location.\n      target_x, target_y = tuple(locations[0])\n\n      # Move next to the primitive (we simply move onto the cell; the\n      # environment will treat the cell as empty once the item is picked up).\n      move_to(target_x, target_y)\n\n      # Pick up the primitive the required number of times.\n      for _ in range(needed_cnt):\n          actions.append(ACTION_USE)\n\n      # After a ``USE`` the primitive disappears from the grid, but we do\n      # not update ``grid`` here \u2013 the plan only needs the sequence of\n      # actions.\n\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  # Finally, go to the workshop and craft the stick.\n  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  if workshop_idx is not None:\n      workshop_cells = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, workshop_idx] > 0)\n      if workshop_cells.size > 0:\n          wx, wy = tuple(workshop_cells[0])\n          move_to(wx, wy)\n          actions.append(ACTION_USE)   # craft at the workshop\n\n  return actions", "env_interactions": 4, "island_id": 7, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
